WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.120
MATTHEW SPALDING: Good evening.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:01.620
Welcome.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.670
It's good to have you.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.800
I'm Matthew Spalding, the
Associate Vice President

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.340
Dean for Hillsdale College,
here in Washington, DC.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.489
Welcome to the Allen
P. Kirby Center

00:00:10.489 --> 00:00:12.280
for Constitutional
Studies and Citizenship,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.540
which is our forward
deployment of Hillsdale College

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.185
in the nation's capitol.

00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:18.810
Hillsdale College,
as most of you know,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.120
is an old institution
based in Michigan,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.020
founded in 1844, that from the
beginning was anti-slavery,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.730
and opened its doors to all,
regardless of race, religion,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.130
or sex.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.110
And today it remains
vigorously independent,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:35.490
among other things not
accepting any government money.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.250
And the reason we do that is
to maintain our independence,

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.100
so that we can remain a trustee
of the Western philosophical

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.100
and theological inheritance,
tracing to Athens

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.880
and Jerusalem, which we believe
find its clearest expression

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.110
in the American idea--

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.320
the American experiment.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.970
Our mission is to
teach those ideas,

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.970
and it follows that we have
always been less interested

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.240
in labels and isms
like conservatism,

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.410
and more interested in thoughts
and actions of politics.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.480
We'd rather focus on
conserving the liberating

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.990
principles of the American
Revolution, for instance.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.120
And so we teach about
politics, constitutionalism,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.987
and the prudence of
statecraft, which

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:16.070
is what we will be teaching
at our graduate school

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.830
we will soon be opening
here in Washington,

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.760
DC to teach those things.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.699
Our speaker today
is Michael Anton,

00:01:23.699 --> 00:01:25.990
who is a lecturer and research
fellow here at the Kirby

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.070
Center.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.900
He received his BA
from the University

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.130
of California at Davis, a
master's of liberal arts

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.460
degree from St. John's
College in Annapolis,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.960
and a master's
degree in government

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.690
from the Claremont
Graduate University.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.400
Prior to joining
Hillsdale College,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.080
he served in the
Trump administration

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.810
as Deputy Assistant
to the President

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.730
for Strategic Communications.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:53.000
He's written for numerous
publications, many of which

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.070
you've seen--

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.080
some famous, some
infamous, I supposs--

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.060
including the Washington
Post, the journal--

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.760
Wall Street Journal,
Weekly Standard, and I

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.753
know he's also a senior fellow
at the Claremont Institute.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.520
The topic this evening, "The End
of Conservatism and the Rebirth

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.940
of Politics."

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.130
Mike Anton.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.070
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.199
MIKE ANTON: This
is kind of scary.

00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:28.740
It reminds me of Peggy Noonan's
little book on speechwriting,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.906
and she said that people's
number one fear typically

00:02:30.906 --> 00:02:32.420
is public speaking.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.650
I've actually had to do it a lot
over the last year and a half

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.692
or so, and I thought it had
gotten kind of easy, you know,

00:02:38.692 --> 00:02:39.650
nothing to worry about.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.290
And so I asked Matt,
well, what do you want?

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.090
Do you want me to script
something out, or just

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:43.780
get up there and wing it?

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.690
Because that's what
I've been doing lately.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.150
He's like, you don't
have to script it out,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.024
but you don't really
want to wing it, either.

00:02:48.024 --> 00:02:49.034
So I wrote an outline.

00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:51.304
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.830
Matt actually came
up with the title.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.950
I came up with something
incredibly witty,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.470
like "The Proper Relationship
Between the General

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.420
and the Particular,"
and I sent that to him.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.587
And he wrote back an email--

00:03:04.587 --> 00:03:06.378
I can't remember-- he
spelled out the word.

00:03:06.378 --> 00:03:08.250
I can't remember if
it was groan or yawn,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.416
but it was one or the other.

00:03:09.416 --> 00:03:09.962
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:09.962 --> 00:03:12.420
And so I said, all right, I
got to think of something else,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:12.600
then.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.410
And he said, what about this?

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.070
That's good.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:14.640
That's good.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.139
That works.

00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:16.880
I can talk about that.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.110
Open with a little warning--

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.275
one of my favorite
teachers-- not a teacher,

00:03:21.275 --> 00:03:23.400
because I never knew him,
but I read all the books,

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.160
and studied his arguments
with care, Leo Strauss, said--

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.810
I think it was in
a letter somewhere,

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.450
he said, the title of a book
or a piece or an article

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.469
should never reflect what's
actually in the contents.

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.760
You need to let the reader
figure that out for himself.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.590
So I'll leave it to you to
figure out whether I actually

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.140
do talk about the
end of conservatism

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.617
and the return of politics.

00:03:41.617 --> 00:03:43.200
One of things you
learned from Strauss

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.970
is, you read all the
platonic dialogues,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.670
and they're all centered around
a what is question, right.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.461
So of course that's
where we have to start.

00:03:49.461 --> 00:03:50.980
What is conservatism?

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.104
I use Lincoln's
definition from the--

00:03:54.104 --> 00:03:55.770
well I'll tell you
where it's from next.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.270
I'm getting ahead
of my own outline.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.160
"What is conservatism?"
said Lincoln, "is it

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.950
not the adherence
to the old and tried

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.745
against the new and untried?"

00:04:03.745 --> 00:04:05.400
It's a decent
definition, I would say,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.530
but notice what Lincoln
does not say there.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.870
He doesn't say
conservatism is true.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:10.920
He doesn't say it's good.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.003
He doesn't say it's right.

00:04:12.003 --> 00:04:13.284
He doesn't say it's just.

00:04:13.284 --> 00:04:15.450
He doesn't say it isn't any
of these things, either,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.560
but he doesn't evaluate
conservatism at all.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.050
He just sort of
tells you what it's

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:26.130
trying to do in terms
of newness and oldness.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:30.770
So I would say we have to step
back conservatism can't really

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.940
be in itself a governing or
a guiding principle for what

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.110
we're trying to do.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.111
For instance, oldness.

00:04:37.111 --> 00:04:38.360
It's the old and tried, right.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.870
Well as a graduate
student friend of mine

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:45.110
once said in a seminar that
got incredibly heated--

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.160
but in a good way--

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.440
he said, you know what,
second only to the family,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.586
slavery is the oldest human
institution in the world.

00:04:51.586 --> 00:04:54.600
I wouldn't say it's good, right.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:55.687
As for it's being tried--

00:04:55.687 --> 00:04:57.270
in Lincoln's terms--
we know it works.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.340
I put that in quotes for-- this
is for the C-Span audience.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.490
It works for the
slaveholder, right?

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.230
Old and tried, therefore
then cannot be a standard

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.337
for the good.

00:05:08.337 --> 00:05:09.420
What's Lincoln doing here?

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.253
He's using rhetoric.

00:05:10.253 --> 00:05:11.850
He's appealing to a
popular prejudice.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.140
The quote comes from
the Cooper Union

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.806
speech, which if you've not read
it, I urge everyone to read it.

00:05:15.806 --> 00:05:18.085
It's very long and complicated.

00:05:18.085 --> 00:05:20.460
It's not his greatest speech,
it's not his most stirring,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.350
but it's his most
intellectually impressive.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.727
His law partner said,
in both awe and lament--

00:05:24.727 --> 00:05:27.060
because I think he felt like
it cost them a lot of money

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.770
because Lincoln wasn't
really working--

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.470
that he spent the better
part of a year working on it.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:36.150
So at the time the speech was
given in February of 1860,

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.610
before Lincoln's
election to president--

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.712
but it's essentially made
him the Republican nominee--

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:43.920
his Republican Party was
being accused of radicalism,

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.350
and seeking to keep slavery
out of the territories.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.391
So they were being called
the anti-conservatives.

00:05:48.391 --> 00:05:50.700
They're the rabble
rousers, the agitators.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.760
His great opponent-- Lincoln's,
that is, in 1858 and '60--

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.440
Stephen Douglas
appealed to the founders

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.900
in support of his position
that slavery should

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.510
be subject to a majority vote.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.850
Douglas said something like,
we should govern ourselves

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.290
in this matter,
as in all matters,

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.749
as did our
forefathers before us.

00:06:05.749 --> 00:06:08.040
So Lincoln said, OK, I'm
going to put that to the test.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.240
I'm going to go research
what they actually

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.440
thought on this question.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.030
He delved through
tons of archives,

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.760
and every single place
where a vote was taken

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.320
on the question of
federal authority

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.120
to exclude slavery
from the territories,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.850
he found that a majority, and
in most cases, a super-majority

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.558
of those founders who
placed a vote said,

00:06:22.558 --> 00:06:24.910
we have the power
to keep it out.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:30.010
So Lincoln turns Douglas's
argument back against itself.

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.380
He destroys Douglas's
appeal to the founders.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.150
They're both making the same
appeal-- we're going back.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:35.020
We're the conservatives.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.030
I'm the conservative-- Lincoln's
saying, I'm the conservative,

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.613
you're saying you're
the conservative.

00:06:38.613 --> 00:06:41.782
Well who's really
right on this question?

00:06:41.782 --> 00:06:44.790
So the argument is rhetorically
brilliant, I would argue,

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.020
but intellectually limited.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.370
What is the American founding?

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:52.210
Is not the new and the
untried, in a certain respect?

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.124
The founders, they
called it an experiment.

00:06:55.124 --> 00:06:57.040
They used a Latin phrase,
novus ordo seclorum,

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.710
a new order for the ages.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.380
Is there a conservatism
that must be open

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:03.565
to risk and to the new?

00:07:03.565 --> 00:07:05.440
I would argue that of
course there should be,

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.060
and I don't think Lincoln
disagreed with me.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.410
What he's doing in that
speech is rhetoric.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.840
That's not an adequate-- that
may be an adequate definition

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:12.506
of conservatism, but
it shows the limits

00:07:12.506 --> 00:07:15.317
of what conservatism
is, or what it can do.

00:07:15.317 --> 00:07:16.900
So I'm going to
mention Strauss again.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:18.310
I think I'm going to mention
him at least one more time,

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.780
just letting you know--

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.791
after this.

00:07:20.791 --> 00:07:22.540
He taught that philosophy
is the awareness

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.192
of permanent
problems, and one such

00:07:24.192 --> 00:07:25.900
is the inherent tension
between the right

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:27.774
and the just on the one
hand, and adventurism

00:07:27.774 --> 00:07:29.596
or newness on the other.

00:07:29.596 --> 00:07:30.970
The Declaration
of Independence--

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:31.990
I'm going to give you
a fairly long quote,

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.440
but I think it's very important.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.800
It says, "prudence
indeed will dictate

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:37.600
that governments
long established

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.770
should not be changed for
light and transient causes.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.390
And accordingly,
all experience hath

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:46.960
shown that mankind are more
disposed to suffer evils

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.420
while evils are sufferable
than to right themselves

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:52.595
by abolishing the forms to
which they are accustomed."

00:07:52.595 --> 00:07:54.970
So the point here is, if you
go too far in one direction,

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.511
you get stasis and
injustice forever.

00:07:56.511 --> 00:07:59.350
Do you just accept
injustice and wrong

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.150
because conservatism
is old and tried,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.587
and anything new is radical?

00:08:03.587 --> 00:08:05.170
You go too far in
the other direction,

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.330
you get utopianism-- every
evil must be righted.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.602
Every problem is urgent.

00:08:08.602 --> 00:08:09.810
We've got to do it right now.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:13.120
And whatever China and eggs
we have to break, not only

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.620
is the cost worth
it, but there's

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:16.300
almost a moral
necessity to doing it.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.795
The only solution
here is prudence.

00:08:20.795 --> 00:08:23.970
You've got to know what to
do in a given circumstance.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:28.745
The American founders-- you
can make a case either way,

00:08:28.745 --> 00:08:30.870
did they need to do what
they did when they did it.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:31.920
They had to argue about that.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.059
There were people who
didn't want to do it,

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:34.530
people who didn't
want the revolution,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:35.700
and they made good arguments.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.299
I'm not saying they were right,
but they made good arguments.

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:38.039
It's not obvious.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:39.419
It requires prudence.

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.190
Prudence requires
knowing the good,

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.360
which requires philosophy.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.640
In other words,
conservatism has to look up

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.800
to something above itself.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.970
The question of what it
conserves absolutely matters.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.670
Conservatism today--
from this point on,

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:56.310
when I say conservatism,
put that in quotes, I

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:02.160
mean kind of an ideology,
a mindset, in some

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.576
respects an industry.

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:05.700
Conservatism likes to say
that it has a philosophy.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.187
I also have that in quotes.

00:09:07.187 --> 00:09:09.270
But once you put an article
in front of the word--

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.061
in other words, once
you say "a philosophy"

00:09:11.061 --> 00:09:13.050
or "the philosophy,"
the philosophy

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.230
of so-and-so-- you've changed
the meaning of the word.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:18.330
Philosophy literally
means, love of wisdom.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:21.900
It's a process of reasoned
investigation that never ends.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.370
When you say, I
have a philosophy,

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.060
you're implying that the
investigation is over,

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.730
that you've figured
everything out.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.044
But have you really?

00:09:29.044 --> 00:09:30.984
I just want to describe
a scene from a book

00:09:30.984 --> 00:09:32.650
that I love that I
urge you all to read.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.720
It's called The Education
of Cyrus, by Xenophon,

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:40.671
and it's the story of the
founder of the Persian Empire,

00:09:40.671 --> 00:09:42.920
Cyrus the Great, although
all the biographical details

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:44.180
are made up, because
Xenophon is really not

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.380
trying to tell you the biography
of the founder of the Persian

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:46.880
Empire.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.680
He's trying to teach
political lessons

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.925
So young Cyrus has
a political project.

00:09:50.925 --> 00:09:53.300
He wants to take Persia and
make it into something else--

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.390
make it bigger, grander,
better, richer, more powerful.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.700
And he runs into
the conservatism

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.460
of the Persian peers, including
his own family, who are

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:02.660
the constitutional monarchs.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:03.951
They don't have a lot of power.

00:10:03.951 --> 00:10:06.740
They're sort of like a
constitutional monarch today,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.940
symbols of the
principles of the regime.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.060
So as he's going
out on his project,

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:17.100
he has a conversation
with his father.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.100
And in this conversation--
by the way, book one,

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.141
chapter six if you want
to read the conversation,

00:10:21.141 --> 00:10:22.520
which I recommend--

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.620
he essentially says, I
know what I'm doing, dad.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:25.795
I know what the good is.

00:10:25.795 --> 00:10:27.170
I know what this
project entails.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:27.680
Don't worry about it.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:28.931
Everything's going to be fine.

00:10:28.931 --> 00:10:30.971
And his father is kind of
a stand in for Socrates

00:10:30.971 --> 00:10:31.940
in this conversation.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.040
He pokes holes in all Cyrus's
arguments, and kind of shows

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:34.610
that he doesn't really.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:37.350
He's not sure Cyrus is wrong,
but Cyrus doesn't really know,

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:41.271
and he has not examined his
own case for what the good is.

00:10:41.271 --> 00:10:43.520
This points to another one
of those insoluble problems

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:44.214
or tensions.

00:10:44.214 --> 00:10:46.130
How do you act when you
don't know what to do,

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:48.830
when you don't know
what is the good?

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.470
So, famously, Socrates
always claimed that he only

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.655
had knowledge of ignorance.

00:10:53.655 --> 00:10:55.530
Fundamentally, the only
true knowledge he had

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.000
was knowledge of ignorance.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.274
He spent a whole lifetime
investigating the noble,

00:10:59.274 --> 00:11:00.690
and the good, and
the just, and he

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.160
claimed that as he died he
still didn't know what it was--

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:04.767
70 years old.

00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:06.600
Then again, if you never
act until you know,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:07.650
you'll never act--

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.840
paralysis.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.320
An analogy that I've used,
which is glib, but not therefore

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.070
inaccurate is, you
can't have a dielectric

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.020
about the noble of the good
when a tank is coming at you.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:17.145
You've got to do something.

00:11:17.145 --> 00:11:19.900
You either got to get out of the
way, or try to stop the tank.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.640
So the important
thing, though, for--

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:28.050
I wrote here, "study the good."

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.090
I'm trying to-- that's
a little bit glib.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.157
What I mean to say at this point
is you have to study the good,

00:11:33.157 --> 00:11:34.740
even with full
knowledge and awareness

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.150
that you may never
get to a completely

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:37.108
adequate understanding.

00:11:37.108 --> 00:11:39.210
If Socrates didn't, then
we probably won't either.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.459
But the fact that you never
get to a full and adequate

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:43.170
analysis, a proof
of what the good is,

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:46.011
doesn't mean you no longer
have an obligation to study it,

00:11:46.011 --> 00:11:47.760
nor does it mean you
can't ever figure out

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.220
anything about what it is.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:53.520
The blessing from Socrates
and from other philosophers

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.800
is to be humble about
what you think you know.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:56.920
Be open to change.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.560
There's a famous quote,
often attributed to Lincoln--

00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.910
and I looked it up
thinking it was Lincoln;

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:01.230
I was going to quote
it as Lincoln--

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.370
but apparently nobody can
prove where it came from.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:05.017
It might have been
John Maynard Keynes.

00:12:05.017 --> 00:12:06.350
We don't even know that, either.

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.177
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:08.177 --> 00:12:08.760
But it's good.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:09.210
It's a good quote.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:10.335
It fits the purpose, right?

00:12:10.335 --> 00:12:12.114
"When the facts change,
I change my mind.

00:12:12.114 --> 00:12:12.780
What do you do?"

00:12:15.722 --> 00:12:17.180
So when conservatives
say they have

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:18.638
a philosophy, what
they really mean

00:12:18.638 --> 00:12:20.860
is that they have an ideology.

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:22.880
Some even use the
phrase, ideology,

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:24.590
in a non-ironic
and positive way.

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:27.200
Maybe it's one of those words
that's morphed over time,

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.222
but initially it
meant something lower,

00:12:31.222 --> 00:12:32.930
something kind of
degraded in philosophy.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.390
What an ideology is a
kind of popular philosophy

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.850
which presents conclusions
that philosophy leaves open.

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:41.586
So the true philosopher of
course disdains ideology.

00:12:41.586 --> 00:12:44.210
But ideology is fine as long as
we recognize it for what it is,

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:45.457
for its limitations.

00:12:45.457 --> 00:12:47.540
I would say it's even
necessary in a practical way

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.746
to solve the what now problem.

00:12:49.746 --> 00:12:51.620
In any political situation,
in any situation,

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:53.840
you've got to deliberate
about what to do.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:58.354
We're not allowed to just
step back and say, well,

00:12:58.354 --> 00:13:00.770
since I can't make up my mind,
let's just not do anything.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:01.894
Life doesn't work that way.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.000
So we need an
ideology, but it always

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.290
has to be open to revision.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:12.080
And that's where I think
conservatism lost its way.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:14.010
Now, I'll just say
this following.

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:14.937
I'm not on Twitter.

00:13:14.937 --> 00:13:16.270
I intend never to be on Twitter.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.853
If you ever find me on Twitter,
come to my house and shoot me.

00:13:18.853 --> 00:13:20.560
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:22.030
Quill pins, inkwells,
and parchment

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.238
are my speed when it comes
to information technology.

00:13:24.238 --> 00:13:25.956
But I do know what a hashtag is.

00:13:25.956 --> 00:13:28.330
And if there were one hashtag
to define conservatism over

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.040
the last decade at
least, maybe longer,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:34.480
it would be #Always1980.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.120
Now I have nothing against 1980.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.830
It was a good year
for me personally.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:41.260
I was 10, so I couldn't vote.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:43.644
And I knew nothing about
politics, but I later learned--

00:13:43.644 --> 00:13:46.060
I came to appreciate Reagan,
and looked back with fondness

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.430
that he won the 1980
election, that he rebuilt

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:50.080
the military, that he cut
taxes, that he jumpstarted

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:50.579
the America.

00:13:50.579 --> 00:13:53.540
All that stuff,
I think, is good.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:57.150
But Reagan's policy solutions
were carefully designed

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.352
and calibrated for his
time by Reagan himself.

00:14:00.352 --> 00:14:02.560
I just-- I don't have this
here, but just as a little

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:06.490
aside, the most famous quote
from Reagan's first inaugural

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.980
is, "government is not the
solution to our problems.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:11.215
Government is the problem."

00:14:11.215 --> 00:14:13.870
But everybody cuts
out-- there's a phrase

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:17.170
before a comma right in front of
that, "in this present crisis."

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.420
"In this present crisis."

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.229
In other words, that's
not a mantra for all time,

00:14:23.229 --> 00:14:25.020
not a mantra for every
crisis, and it maybe

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.010
it's not a mantra
for non-crisis.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.480
Anyway, conservatism
came to believe

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.040
that Reagan's
policies would work

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.690
for all time-- were timeless.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:34.760
Some probably are, but not all.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.650
And then there's the
matter of priorities.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:40.400
When the top marginal tax rate
is 70%, as it was in 1980,

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.210
tax cuts are very high priority.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:45.880
When they are in the
30s, not so much.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:48.816
Or if you want, instead of
1980, you could pick 1955.

00:14:48.816 --> 00:14:49.690
Why do I choose that?

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:52.830
Because it's the year of the
foundation of National Review.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.540
What is National Review doing?

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.580
Fundamentally-- no, you laugh.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.490
I'm not here to bury or
criticize National Review.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:05.177
It's certainly not in
its 1955 dispensation.

00:15:05.177 --> 00:15:06.260
What did National Review--

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:07.635
I mean, Buckley's
big achievement

00:15:07.635 --> 00:15:10.840
there, aside from giving a
voice and venue to writers

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:19.100
who didn't have an outlet, was
to see these disparate threads

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:19.990
that had maybe--

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:20.880
that weren't talking
to each other,

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.340
but maybe had something in
common, and put them together.

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:23.870
This came to be
called fusionism,

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:27.044
and the three threads were the
traditional, religious slash

00:15:27.044 --> 00:15:29.210
conservatives who were very
concerned with morality,

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.220
the economic conservatives
and the libertarians,

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:34.760
and the foreign policy hawks.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:37.260
Again, necessary
to do for its time,

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:39.200
and a spectacular
achievement in fact.

00:15:42.334 --> 00:15:43.750
So I'm not here
to criticize them.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:47.484
I'm here to criticize us,
and yes, that includes me.

00:15:47.484 --> 00:15:48.900
I was once a
movement conservative

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.100
just like a lot of people
in this room-- maybe most

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:52.290
of the people in this room.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.130
So this is very much
a self criticism.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:59.240
My point is that Reagan,
Buckley, these people who met--

00:15:59.240 --> 00:15:59.900
and Lincoln--

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:01.970
the people who met the
crises of their time

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:05.000
did what the conservative
movement as I've seen it

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.710
hasn't been able to do in
the last 10 or 20 years.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.920
That is they went to a
reservoir of deeper ideas,

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:13.887
and they used them to
examine their own time.

00:16:13.887 --> 00:16:14.720
What did Lincoln do?

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.870
He went back to the
founding founding.

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:19.767
The founders were asking
fundamental questions.

00:16:19.767 --> 00:16:21.100
What is the purpose of politics?

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:22.210
What is the good society?

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:23.830
What does justice require?

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:25.760
What is justice itself?

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:31.020
I want to tell a very
short anecdote there.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:34.940
So you know, I said,
every platonic dialogue

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:35.860
is a what is question.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:37.300
That's of course, the
question of the Republic--

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:38.350
what is justice.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:41.350
So when Harvey
Mansfield was teaching

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:44.240
in Berkeley in the
early '60s, Leo Strauss

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.190
spent a semester at Stanford.

00:16:48.197 --> 00:16:50.530
People who knew both of them,
Harry Jaffa in particular,

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:53.134
said, you've got to go see
this guy as much as you can.

00:16:53.134 --> 00:16:55.800
Mansfield would drive across the
Bay and see Strauss every week,

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.990
and they'd read The Republic,
and with some other students.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:16:59.448
And one of the
first things Strauss

00:16:59.448 --> 00:17:01.766
apparently asked him was
(HIGH PITCHED GERMAN ACCENT)

00:17:01.766 --> 00:17:04.865
what is the question at the
center of this dialogue?

00:17:04.865 --> 00:17:07.240
(IN OWN VOICE) And the room
was silent, and they're like,

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:07.930
he's too important.

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.513
What if I get the wrong answer
in front of this brilliant man?

00:17:10.513 --> 00:17:11.364
Nobody talked.

00:17:11.364 --> 00:17:14.440
The clock ticked by, and
finally Mansfield said,

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:15.310
what is justice?

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.410
And Strauss says, (WITH ACCENT)
yes, that's the right answer.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:19.035
(IN OWN VOICE) And
he felt so relieved.

00:17:19.035 --> 00:17:20.170
I got the right answer.

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:22.450
This genius recognizes that
I got the right answer.

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:23.829
It was an easy question.

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:26.079
He thought maybe there was
a trick in there somewhere.

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:26.380
OK.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:28.630
We go back to the founding,
we get fundamental questions.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.170
We go back to 1980,
what do we get?

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:32.336
We get tax cuts, enterprise
zones, small government,

00:17:32.336 --> 00:17:33.670
being tough on the Russians.

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.295
Not that these are
bad things, but they

00:17:35.295 --> 00:17:39.490
don't address the fundamental
issues of 2016 or 2018.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:43.090
So I'm going to give
you another quote.

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:46.360
This is the chapter
title of book three,

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.390
chapter one of the
discourses of Machiavelli.

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:52.240
It is entitled, "if one
wishes a sect or a republic

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:54.970
to live long, it is
necessary to draw it back

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:56.844
off and toward its beginnings."

00:17:56.844 --> 00:17:58.510
I'm not going to tell
you what he really

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:02.860
means by that because it's
a lot meaner than it sounds.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:04.870
But for conservatives,
the beginning

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.325
is 1980, or maybe 1955.

00:18:07.325 --> 00:18:09.700
For some, I think, it even
moved further forward in time.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.760
And there's a pretty big wing of
the conservative movement which

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:13.720
now would say the
whole 1980 platform is

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.351
much too mean and harsh.

00:18:15.351 --> 00:18:17.350
They'd maybe point to
compassionate conservative

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:19.100
in 2000.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:21.100
One of the things
conservativism has done lately

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.100
is it's jettisoned a lot of
what is best in its useful past.

00:18:26.896 --> 00:18:28.270
So I would say
the real beginning

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.391
for us is the founders--

00:18:30.391 --> 00:18:32.890
the American founding, and the
principles which underlay it,

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:35.440
some of which are contemporary
to its time, some of which

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.440
belong to the early modern
period, some of which

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.150
go all the way back
to the classical era.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.430
Now conservatives all
agree-- they say they all

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.638
agree-- the one thing they
will say they all agree on

00:18:44.638 --> 00:18:46.390
is they love the founding.

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:47.680
They love the founding.

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:49.720
But like Cyrus and the
good, in the conversation

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.678
with his father, they
think they understand it,

00:18:51.678 --> 00:18:54.220
but I question whether
they really do.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:55.900
Now this is another
Strauss quote.

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:57.720
I think is the last one.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.640
He wrote a book in
1949 called On Tyranny.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:02.830
1949, it's four years after
the end of World War II.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:04.246
Stalin's conquered
half of Europe.

00:19:04.246 --> 00:19:08.027
He's a very bad man, and the
Soviet Empire is going strong.

00:19:08.027 --> 00:19:09.610
Strauss writes an
analysis of tyranny,

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:11.500
and he makes an indictment
against his fellow political

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:11.999
scientists.

00:19:11.999 --> 00:19:15.330
He says, "when we were brought
face to face with tyranny,

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.250
with a kind of tyranny
that surpassed the boldest

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.250
imagination of the most
powerful thinkers of the past,

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.251
our political science
failed to recognize it."

00:19:23.251 --> 00:19:25.000
He's essentially saying,
if you're doctor,

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.100
and I bring in to you a
terminal cancer patient,

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.110
and you can't even tell
me that he has cancer,

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:30.890
then you don't know
what you're doing.

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:33.254
Well, I would flip that
onto the conservatives.

00:19:33.254 --> 00:19:34.670
The American people
were presented

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:36.740
with the most conservative
founder-like presidential

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:37.910
candidate in a generation.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.580
Our conservatives
failed to recognize it.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.530
Now that, people, is
going to sound crazy.

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:45.610
Trump is the least
conservative Republican ever.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:47.120
He deviates from
these principles

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:48.020
all across the board.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:49.610
How can you say that?

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:53.480
Well, what do you think
the founding is about?

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:54.500
I think I know what--

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:55.940
I'm going to guess at
what's in the heads of a lot

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:57.106
of the people in this room--

00:19:57.106 --> 00:20:00.480
liberty, freedom, rights,
equality, constitution.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:01.480
All those are essential.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:03.210
No doubt about it.

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:05.420
But the real-- the
core of the founding--

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.910
what the founders are
trying to do is protection--

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:09.576
the protection of
rights, the protection

00:20:09.576 --> 00:20:11.510
of persons, protection
of property, protection

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:12.496
of the country itself--

00:20:12.496 --> 00:20:14.620
of the physical country
itself, of its communities,

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:16.000
of its industries, and so on.

00:20:18.634 --> 00:20:21.050
If we go back to the founding,
the pre-founding beginning,

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.990
the true beginning, we find
another fundamental philosophic

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:27.285
distinction, that between
mere life and the good life.

00:20:27.285 --> 00:20:29.160
You can't have the latter
without the former.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.046
The founders understood this.

00:20:31.046 --> 00:20:33.050
I'll just make a small
aside, which I left out

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:34.841
because it goes down
kind of a rabbit hole,

00:20:34.841 --> 00:20:37.340
but this is one of those
ancient to modern distinctions

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.105
is the ancients and the
moderns would say, of course,

00:20:40.105 --> 00:20:41.230
you have to have mere life.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:43.390
Base protection is fundamental.

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:44.875
No disagreement there.

00:20:44.875 --> 00:20:47.000
How much does the state,
or the polls, or whatever,

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.250
get involved in the good life?

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.630
The ancients would say, a
lot, right, maybe almost

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.060
overwhelmingly.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.670
There's a line floating
around out there that came up

00:20:55.670 --> 00:20:57.430
on an email list I'm on.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.840
It goes something like,
the ancient formula is,

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:02.637
anything the law does
not command, it forbids.

00:21:02.637 --> 00:21:04.970
Whereas the modern formula
is, anything the law does not

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:06.556
forbid, it permits.

00:21:06.556 --> 00:21:08.180
The founders would
say the state should

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:09.576
have a lot less--
it should leave

00:21:09.576 --> 00:21:10.950
you free to pursue
the good life,

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.200
have a lot less involvement,
but not zero involvement.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.410
They were not libertarians.

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:18.039
They were strongly for
laws inculcating virtue,

00:21:18.039 --> 00:21:19.830
promoting religion,
and so on and so forth.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.080
Now I'm not saying
that our conservatives

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.567
would deny any of this.

00:21:25.567 --> 00:21:26.150
They wouldn't.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:27.704
They'd say, of course.

00:21:27.704 --> 00:21:28.854
Duh.

00:21:28.854 --> 00:21:30.270
And I do need to
give them credit.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:32.061
They've done some great
work on this theme.

00:21:32.061 --> 00:21:34.940
In particular, what
movement has done more

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:38.270
to protect lives,
property, and society

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:40.820
than the great crime-fighting
movement that was essentially

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:43.340
launched by conservative
intellectuals-- the Manhattan

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:46.190
Institute-- and put in
place by conservative mayors

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:49.489
and governors since the
late '80s and early '90s.

00:21:49.489 --> 00:21:51.530
It's one of the great
conservative policy success

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:55.360
stories of my lifetime--
maybe the single greatest.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.130
But conservatism
failed to protect us

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.770
in three fundamental ways--

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:01.667
on immigration, trade,
and foreign policy.

00:22:01.667 --> 00:22:03.000
Now this is not a policy speech.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:04.920
I'm not going to go into
this in great detail,

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.753
although I'm sure you'll
all have questions,

00:22:06.753 --> 00:22:08.199
and I'll have the
answer to them.

00:22:08.199 --> 00:22:09.990
And I do have strong
thoughts on all three,

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:11.323
but I mostly want to answer why.

00:22:11.323 --> 00:22:12.840
Why didn't they recognize it?

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.030
So yet another philosophic
distinction, that

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.790
between the form and the matter.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.460
In political terms, the form
is the regime or the principle,

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:24.030
the matter is the people, the
land, the stuff, the buildings,

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:26.849
the industries, the resources.

00:22:26.849 --> 00:22:28.390
There's a line in
Francis Bacon where

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:30.730
he quotes the dangers of
the little philosophy.

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.880
"Little philosophy doth
incline men's minds to atheism,

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.440
but depth in the
subject bringeth

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:36.515
them back around to God."

00:22:36.515 --> 00:22:37.390
I think that's exact.

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:38.600
I'm not sure.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:39.730
I didn't write it down.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:41.770
So conservatives, they
got a little philosophy

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:44.290
about first principle, and
they fell in love with it.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:45.831
And essentially what
I think happened

00:22:45.831 --> 00:22:48.052
is they sloganized the
founding principles.

00:22:48.052 --> 00:22:49.510
They love to quote
the Declaration.

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:51.218
We all quote the
Declaration, but we only

00:22:51.218 --> 00:22:52.350
quote the second paragraph.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.600
Everybody-- "we hold these
truths to be self-evident."

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:55.350
Everybody knows the
second paragraph.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:56.970
Do you know the first paragraph?

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:58.972
I'll read it.

00:22:58.972 --> 00:23:00.690
When you hear me
emphasize my voice,

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:02.900
and sort of yell a little,
that means it's bolded.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:04.392
When it's on the page, right?

00:23:04.392 --> 00:23:05.850
"When in the course
of human events

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.915
it becomes necessary
for one people

00:23:07.915 --> 00:23:10.290
to dissolve the political
bands which have connected them

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:13.920
with another, and to assume
among the powers of the earth

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:17.392
that separate and equal station
to which the laws of nature

00:23:17.392 --> 00:23:19.350
and nature's God entitle
them, a decent respect

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:20.730
to the opinions
of mankind require

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.938
that they should declare
the causes which compel them

00:23:22.938 --> 00:23:24.354
to that separation."

00:23:24.354 --> 00:23:26.520
So what is the founding,
fundamentally-- or at least

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:27.311
at the basic level?

00:23:27.311 --> 00:23:29.460
It's when we declared
ourselves people.

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.410
The principles of
paragraph two are declared

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.434
to be true for all mankind.

00:23:33.434 --> 00:23:35.850
But the founders would not
pretend that they had the power

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.497
to apply them to all
mankind, nor would they say,

00:23:38.497 --> 00:23:40.080
even if they had
that power, that they

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.430
had any right to do so.

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:43.404
Remember, the separate
and equal station.

00:23:43.404 --> 00:23:44.820
It's right there
in paragraph one.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:47.028
Nations are entitled to a
separate and equal station,

00:23:47.028 --> 00:23:51.500
"entitled by nature
and nature's God."

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.630
Lincoln called the
second paragraph--

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:56.630
Lincoln famously referred
to the Declaration itself

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.830
as a merely
revolutionary document.

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:01.100
It's just stating fact.

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:03.489
And he says, absolutely could
have written that document

00:24:03.489 --> 00:24:05.030
without the second
paragraph at all--

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:08.400
didn't have to put any
political theory in it.

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:11.530
But it's there to
state for all time

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:13.730
the basis of
political legitimacy.

00:24:13.730 --> 00:24:16.994
This is how we define justice,
and how we base legitimacy

00:24:16.994 --> 00:24:18.160
in our regime going forward.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.980
It will no longer be
on the divine right

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:23.092
of kings, or
aristocratic principles,

00:24:23.092 --> 00:24:24.550
or any of the things
that prevailed

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:25.466
in Europe at the time.

00:24:25.466 --> 00:24:28.810
And Lincoln also said that
statement was in there in order

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:31.810
to be forever a stumbling
block to future tyrants

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:34.060
and future oppression,
that it would

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:36.460
galvanize the minds
of the people,

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:40.540
and make them more aware,
and more apt to fight back

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:44.267
should such oppressors arise.

00:24:44.267 --> 00:24:46.100
So I would say the
reason that conservatives

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:48.350
missed the Trump phenomenon
is because they don't really

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:50.030
understand trade and
immigration, which

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:53.330
is because they don't
understand first principle.

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:54.319
Or maybe they forgot.

00:24:54.319 --> 00:24:54.860
I don't know.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:56.960
I'm trying to be a
little more generous.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:59.870
They recoil at any talk as
Americans as a separate people,

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:01.500
because they think this
violates some first principle.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:02.930
Now that's maybe a little
bit of an overstatement,

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:04.596
but it's getting more
radical out there,

00:25:04.596 --> 00:25:07.460
I think, with the
immigration debate.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.100
They forgot the proper
relationship of the general--

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.450
the form-- to the
particular matters.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:16.620
Oh, there I got my title
into my lecture anyway.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.290
So on this question, the
ancients understand--

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:21.970
Plato, and Aristotle, and
the founders-- they agree.

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:23.220
There is only one best regime.

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:25.290
It's true for all
times and places.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:29.660
It's not always practicable,
meaning possible to set up,

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:31.760
or even if you could set
it up, to make it last.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.100
And in fact, it very
rarely will be practicable.

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:37.852
It's practicability
depends on the matter,

00:25:37.852 --> 00:25:40.060
whether the given matter is
capable of sustaining it.

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.200
And you all know the very
famous Ben Franklin quote.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:43.890
He walks out of the
Constitutional Convention.

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:45.740
What did you give us,
Mr. Franklin, a monarchy

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:46.130
or a republic?

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:47.720
He says, "a republic,
if you can keep it."

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:49.261
That's an awareness
of the importance

00:25:49.261 --> 00:25:52.820
of matter to the regime, that
the matter has to fit the form,

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:54.454
and vice versa.

00:25:54.454 --> 00:25:56.120
The fact that there
are universal truths

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:58.820
does not obviate another
fact, namely that all politics

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.264
is inherently particular.

00:26:00.264 --> 00:26:02.180
There will always be
separate cities, nations,

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:04.430
and countries entitled to
separate and equal stations.

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:08.940
One of the points
of the Cyropaedia

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:11.884
is to point out the limits
of that understanding.

00:26:11.884 --> 00:26:14.300
And essentially Xenophon, just
like Plato in The Republic,

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:15.950
is making sort of
the same point.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:19.280
Ultimately the distinction
between citizen and non-citizen

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:20.930
may be arbitrary
in the sense that--

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.040
where the line is drawn.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:23.950
There is not always
a clear line.

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:25.577
But that there will
always be a line

00:26:25.577 --> 00:26:27.410
is a fundamental fact
of human nature, which

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:29.870
is why one world government
is both impossible and

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:31.940
undesirable.

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:33.339
Now this may definitely be--

00:26:33.339 --> 00:26:35.630
this is definitely glib, and
it may be slightly unfair,

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:38.390
but not therefore
wholly inaccurate.

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:42.050
I think conservatives allowed
their misunderstanding of form

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:44.900
to convince them that principle
requires globalization.

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:48.050
We are all Tom Friedman now
in the conservative movement.

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:50.674
Certainly they think
it requires free trade.

00:26:50.674 --> 00:26:53.090
They think there's some economic
principle that absolutely

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:54.230
requires free trade,
and protection

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:55.440
is the worst thing
you could possibly do,

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:57.523
and it's almost an affront
to the national dignity

00:26:57.523 --> 00:26:58.847
of every American.

00:26:58.847 --> 00:26:59.930
This is kind of hilarious.

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:01.804
The very first law the
United States Congress

00:27:01.804 --> 00:27:03.330
passed after it
became operational

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:05.120
was the Tariff Act of 1789.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:06.620
Now it wasn't exactly
the first law.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:09.652
They passed a couple of sort
of housekeeping, managerial--

00:27:09.652 --> 00:27:11.360
but the first substantive
law they passed

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:12.570
was the Tariff Act of 1789.

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:14.070
You can't appeal
to Lincoln, either.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.290
He was a thoroughgoing
protectionist,

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:17.480
and the entire Lincoln-inspired
Republican Party

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:19.520
that dominated American
politics from 1865

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:21.860
until the progressive era
was also protectionist.

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:23.817
So I'm not denouncing
free trade.

00:27:23.817 --> 00:27:24.900
I'm saying, it's a policy.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:26.060
It depends on the context.

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:27.410
There are times in
which it's good for us,

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:29.210
and there are times in which
maybe it isn't working out.

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:31.418
It certainly worked out
extremely well for the United

00:27:31.418 --> 00:27:34.844
States from 1945 to 1965, maybe
you could stretch that to '75.

00:27:34.844 --> 00:27:37.010
Actually Reagan understood
the dangers of free trade

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.350
better than the conservatives
today who appeal to him,

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:42.310
and introduced a number
of protectionist measures,

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:44.420
and arguably saved
the US auto industry

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:48.409
by doing things to
the Japanese auto

00:27:48.409 --> 00:27:50.700
industry that conservatives
today would find completely

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.640
[INAUDIBLE]

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:55.700
The founders on
immigration-- similar.

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:57.492
They knew it depended
on the context.

00:27:57.492 --> 00:27:59.450
In the founding era, you
see a lot of arguments

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:02.452
from leading founders saying,
we have this giant continent.

00:28:02.452 --> 00:28:04.160
They didn't have the
whole continent yet,

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:05.180
but if you think
about it-- and I

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.430
think on the eve of the
revolution of the 13 colonies,

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:08.970
there were about
3 million people.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:09.970
And they said, that's
not enough people.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:10.640
We can't even hold it.

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:12.680
We can't hold this land
with that many people.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:14.540
We need more people for our
own safety and security.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:15.650
They made this
argument, immigration

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:17.360
is good for the
existing citizenry.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:19.250
Let's welcome in some people.

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:21.140
But they're asking
the right question--

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:24.050
do we, the people,
need more people?

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:25.555
Sometimes we do.

00:28:25.555 --> 00:28:27.680
The failure to understand
principle, I would argue,

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.887
also has real
rhetorical implications.

00:28:31.887 --> 00:28:33.970
I'm hearing this more and
more from conservatives.

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:35.724
Any time you bring
up the particular,

00:28:35.724 --> 00:28:38.390
their immediate go-to, it's like
just a gut reaction, or a rash,

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.515
or something, is identity
politics, and that's bad.

00:28:40.515 --> 00:28:41.598
That's what the left does.

00:28:41.598 --> 00:28:42.110
It's bad.

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:43.400
Only talk about universals.

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:45.620
Anytime you talk about anything
particular and not universal,

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.161
you're engaging in
identity politics.

00:28:49.427 --> 00:28:51.260
This misunderstanding
of principle, I think,

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:53.780
is weaponized by the left, and
used against the conservatives.

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:55.490
And they still haven't
really caught on.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:56.735
I caught on.

00:28:56.735 --> 00:28:58.324
I'm trying to help them.

00:28:58.324 --> 00:28:59.240
They don't to hear it.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:01.700
You can trust me.

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:03.620
Your principles-- the
left will say to them,

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.430
your principles mean that
you must be for open borders.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:07.200
And they'll go--

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:08.741
[SIGHING] they don't
want to believe,

00:29:08.741 --> 00:29:10.880
you know, but they think--
they tie themselves

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:12.880
in knots trying to think
of a principled answer,

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:14.162
or they give in and agree.

00:29:14.162 --> 00:29:15.620
The founders would
just simply say,

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:17.450
what are you talking about?

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.130
We the people get
to decide that.

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:21.205
That's the principle
of republicanism.

00:29:21.205 --> 00:29:22.580
And the founders
would also say--

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.621
and this is an argument
that conservatives really

00:29:24.621 --> 00:29:27.470
get super upset about, is
that it depends on the matter,

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:29.479
or the suitability
for republicanism

00:29:29.479 --> 00:29:30.770
of the people you're admitting.

00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:33.170
They make this
argument very openly.

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:35.090
We're doing an
experiment, right.

00:29:35.090 --> 00:29:36.923
Remember, when the
founders found the United

00:29:36.923 --> 00:29:38.430
States of America, the lat--

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:41.442
there's really only
one arguably one--

00:29:41.442 --> 00:29:43.400
arguably two, I would
say, successful republics

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:44.637
in the world at that time.

00:29:44.637 --> 00:29:46.220
Switzerland, or the
Swiss Confederacy,

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:48.050
and the Netherlands,
and the Netherlands

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:53.230
is nominally ruled by a
Hapsburg monarch out of Madrid.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:56.690
So the last experience that
the West had with republics

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:59.274
would be the Renaissance,
and they were all disasters.

00:29:59.274 --> 00:30:01.940
The founders have to make-- this
was The Federalist 1-6 was them

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:03.740
making a case for why
republicanism is not

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:07.190
a complete failure in human
terms, but it can be revived,

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:08.870
and it ought to be revived.

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:11.280
But then they say, if it's
going to be sustained,

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:13.488
we have to make sure that
the character of the people

00:30:13.488 --> 00:30:15.260
remains republican,
which they say

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:17.759
has a lot to do with what we're
going to do on immigration--

00:30:17.759 --> 00:30:20.340
sorry, on education policy--
but on immigration policy.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:24.810
Well conservatives
cannot bear that thought.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.730
They think it's ruled
out by their principles,

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:28.710
and as a result they concede
a huge amount of vital ground

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:30.251
to the left, and
they lose arguments,

00:30:30.251 --> 00:30:34.010
which I think they/we have
been doing for a while.

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:39.050
Another way we lose is to
accept the tyranny of expertise.

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.026
The modern left, drawing
from Hegel and others,

00:30:41.026 --> 00:30:42.650
asserts that there
are no fundamentally

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:43.880
political questions,
that is, just

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.005
questions which there's no
necessary right or wrong

00:30:46.005 --> 00:30:49.130
answer, or that is to say,
correct or incorrect answer.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:50.970
There might be a
right or wrong answer.

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:53.040
There are only
issues or problems

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:55.160
that are correct or incorrect.

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:57.890
And naturally, the left
has all the solutions,

00:30:57.890 --> 00:30:59.780
and they have the
research university,

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:02.630
which I analogize to the
pre-revolutionary monasteries

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:05.660
of France, which Napoleon
spent a serious amount of time

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:09.020
and effort breaking
up for good reason.

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:11.926
And the analogy is more
apt than you think.

00:31:11.926 --> 00:31:12.800
I mean, they're rich.

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:15.340
I didn't coin this
term, but I wish I had.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:17.090
I mean, I'm all for
Hillsdale's endowment.

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:18.830
May it grow in perpetuity.

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:21.470
But colleges that are
teaching bad things

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:24.980
and doing bad things to the
society, we have to recognize.

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:27.870
They're hedge funds with
these little schools attached.

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:31.419
And another analogy to
pre-revolutionary France--

00:31:31.419 --> 00:31:33.710
remember, one of the reasons
that the French Revolution

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:35.750
happened is because the nobility
didn't have to pay taxes.

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:36.770
The richest people
in the society

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:37.811
didn't have to pay taxes.

00:31:37.811 --> 00:31:39.230
Universities don't pay taxes.

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:42.260
The guys who manage their hedge
funds pay the so-called two

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:43.030
and 20--

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:44.759
no, sorry, the
investor pays that.

00:31:44.759 --> 00:31:46.550
And they get the carried
interest loophole.

00:31:46.550 --> 00:31:48.650
So they make a lot
of money, and they

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.650
don't have to pay
ordinary income tax on it.

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:53.150
I call that the tax
exempt nobility.

00:31:53.150 --> 00:31:54.899
It's certainly the tax
privilege nobility.

00:31:54.899 --> 00:31:57.610
It's not good.

00:31:57.610 --> 00:32:00.630
So conservatism too often
foolishly accepts this,

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:04.377
that, OK, all these are
questions of expertise,

00:32:04.377 --> 00:32:06.460
so we just have to come
up with a better argument.

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:07.870
Well, we're going to do
a better policy paper,

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:10.161
we're going to run the numbers,
and we're going to win,

00:32:10.161 --> 00:32:14.205
so then our expertise will
get to decide the solution.

00:32:14.205 --> 00:32:16.580
But that's not the game the
left is playing against them.

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:17.830
I don't think they realize it.

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:19.621
It's the game they tell
us they're playing,

00:32:19.621 --> 00:32:22.340
and then I think we are
dumb enough to believe it.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:24.340
We're losing the rhetorical
war because we don't

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:25.660
understand first principle.

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.160
We don't know how
to apply principle

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:28.990
to the challenges of our time--

00:32:28.990 --> 00:32:32.290
and because we came to mistake
policies for principles.

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:38.060
Almost done.

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:39.980
There is a conservative
reaction against this,

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:42.800
which I think is healthy
and good, but it's inchoate,

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:45.200
and there's a problem
at its core, which

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:47.979
is that the people who see
the problems with current--

00:32:47.979 --> 00:32:49.520
or a lot of them
who see the problems

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:53.190
with current conservatism
say that the problem

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:54.250
is the principle.

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:56.750
They just equate principles
with universalism, and they say,

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:58.625
so the solution is just
to abandon principle,

00:32:58.625 --> 00:33:02.504
and we're just going to go back
to the particular altogether.

00:33:02.504 --> 00:33:03.920
It always tramples
the particular.

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:06.575
I say, first of all,
that's logically not true.

00:33:06.575 --> 00:33:08.200
I could explain it,
but it would take--

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:10.570
there's a bit of a
proof-- it takes a bit--

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:12.370
so I'll skip that.

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:16.270
But also, this is important, the
founders didn't believe that.

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:18.160
So to those in the
traditionalist right,

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:19.120
or whatever we
want to call them,

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:21.550
who invoke American history,
customs, traditions, et cetera,

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:22.330
I sympathize all the time.

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:23.950
I want to preserve all
of those things too.

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:25.720
The fundamental question,
what is conservatism

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:26.590
trying to conserve?

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:28.298
Those are some of the
things conservatism

00:33:28.298 --> 00:33:30.220
ought to be trying to serve.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:32.290
But tradition fundamentally
is about the passion

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:33.769
of love of one's own.

00:33:33.769 --> 00:33:35.310
It's the problem
with traditionalism,

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:37.470
is it can't give you an account
of why tradition is good.

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:38.650
Some traditions are good.

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:40.380
We should defend
them on that basis.

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:42.880
But if you don't know what the
good is, or have no idea what

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.650
the good is, or if you deny
the existence of the good,

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:46.870
you have no basis on which
to say tradition is good.

00:33:46.870 --> 00:33:48.615
That's not my point here though.

00:33:48.615 --> 00:33:50.740
My point is, the founding
is-- if love of one's own

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:52.990
is the passion that the
traditionalist want to take us

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:56.450
back to, away from some sort
of radical particularism, well

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:01.120
the founding is our own, so it's
self-contradictory to denounce

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:04.390
the founding principles,
or principles in general,

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:06.640
and then say you want
to appeal to-- it's also

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:09.489
self-defeating and
unnecessary-- and then say,

00:34:09.489 --> 00:34:10.960
but we want to
appeal to America,

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:13.550
and America is what
we're trying to save.

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:14.139
All right.

00:34:14.139 --> 00:34:15.730
So everybody-- you're
always supposed

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:18.669
to on, what do we do now,
to which I would say,

00:34:18.669 --> 00:34:19.210
I don't know.

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:20.444
But I have some answers.

00:34:20.444 --> 00:34:22.610
The first task is to
understand principle correctly,

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:23.170
and that's within reach.

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:24.340
We can do it, because
there's books out there that

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:25.780
explain it, and there's
teachers who know it.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:26.739
There's no reason
why we have to get

00:34:26.739 --> 00:34:28.060
first principle in such a mess.

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:29.144
There's no reason for it--

00:34:29.144 --> 00:34:31.393
at least there's no reason
why we can't clean that up.

00:34:31.393 --> 00:34:34.070
That's achievable, and it's
achievable in the near term.

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:35.734
The second is to
just not be afraid.

00:34:35.734 --> 00:34:37.150
Once we learn the
principles, stop

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:38.774
conceding rhetorical
ground to the left

00:34:38.774 --> 00:34:41.100
as if they understand our
principles better than we do,

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:42.899
and keep losing.

00:34:42.899 --> 00:34:44.690
The third is we need
to have honest debates

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:48.792
amongst ourselves about how
to apply principle to policy,

00:34:48.792 --> 00:34:50.750
and we have to recognize
that those debates are

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:51.409
political debates.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:52.908
They're not debates
about expertise,

00:34:52.908 --> 00:34:55.250
about correct and false.

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:57.740
They are, in a philosophic
and perhaps theological sense,

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:00.460
debates about right
and wrong, but we

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:04.410
need to understand
that in a sense,

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:06.750
the political sphere has been
either taken away from us

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:09.570
or vastly constricted, and
we need to get it back.

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:13.742
So we can look to the founders
for the first half for sure.

00:35:13.742 --> 00:35:16.200
We can look to the founders
for the second-- be not afraid,

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:17.330
and fight back.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:19.121
We can't really look
to them for the third.

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:25.370
Their ideas will not supply us
with recipes for today's use.

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:29.150
Only we living today can
possibly find the solution

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:31.010
to the problems of today.

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:33.980
They can give us the basis on
which to find those solutions,

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:35.920
and that's where we
need to start looking.

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:40.580
And I think ultimately that's
why I'm here at Hillsdale,

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:41.750
working here now--

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:43.400
dream job, very
happy to be here.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:45.802
I look forward to doing
it for many years to come.

00:35:45.802 --> 00:35:47.510
I think that's what
the college is trying

00:35:47.510 --> 00:35:48.968
to do in a broad
sense, and I would

00:35:48.968 --> 00:35:51.800
include in all of that,
which I left out of the talk

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:54.789
specifically, but not just
America, but the whole West--

00:35:54.789 --> 00:35:56.830
the whole West-- the whole
civilizational project

00:35:56.830 --> 00:35:58.704
of the West is what
we're trying to conserve,

00:35:58.704 --> 00:36:00.745
fight for, correctly understand.

00:36:03.094 --> 00:36:04.260
Whether we're going to win--

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:05.885
we can win the
argument, and then we're

00:36:05.885 --> 00:36:07.020
going to win the fight.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:08.852
I don't know.

00:36:08.852 --> 00:36:10.810
I didn't write this part
down, but I will quote

00:36:10.810 --> 00:36:12.112
Machiavelli one more time.

00:36:12.112 --> 00:36:14.070
He lays out the sort of
fundamental alternative

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:16.653
between two courses of action
in Discourses, book one, chapter

00:36:16.653 --> 00:36:18.589
six, and he says reason
can't really make

00:36:18.589 --> 00:36:19.880
a case for either one of these.

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:22.680
You know, you could argue
this, you could argue that.

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:25.210
It's got a strong case for
and against for both of them.

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:28.470
So he says, take the honorable
course, and do this one.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:29.970
Take the honorable
course and fight,

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:33.270
even if you think
you're going to lose.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:34.648
I'll end there.

00:36:34.648 --> 00:36:38.141
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:50.322 --> 00:36:52.113
SPEAKER 1: We have time
for some questions,

00:36:52.113 --> 00:36:55.107
and we have a couple of
microphones [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:55.107 --> 00:36:56.604
in the back.

00:36:56.604 --> 00:36:59.099
So, right there.

00:37:05.846 --> 00:37:07.220
AUDIENCE: Mr.
Anton, thank you so

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:10.030
much for coming and speaking.

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:13.070
Definitely thank you again.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:15.710
What I want to ask about is,
you mentioned Mr. Lincoln.

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:19.820
Mr. Lincoln was someone who
said that Henry Clay was

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:24.200
his beau of an ideal
statesman, and yet Henry Clay

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:27.140
would have been horrified
by the Homestead Act

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:28.940
that Mr. Lincoln signed.

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:32.540
It's contrary to his policy of
how to form land in the West.

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:35.720
So my question is, in terms
of in the administration

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:40.130
of public policy from a
perspective of conservatism,

00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:44.240
but moreover trueness
to first principles,

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:48.380
when does one in a sense
change one's principles--

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:50.770
not change principles, sorry--

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:56.300
change how one has
planned to administer

00:37:56.300 --> 00:37:59.690
and work within
government versus what

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:02.950
is the plans of the times.

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:05.350
MIKE ANTON: I'm not sure
I understood the question.

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:08.540
I'll give you a little
pseudo-autobiographical

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:09.040
account.

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:10.166
I was always a free trader.

00:38:10.166 --> 00:38:12.206
I thought the conservative
principle demanded it.

00:38:12.206 --> 00:38:13.840
I didn't study much
economics, but what

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:16.606
I studied seemed like,
as a matter of expertise,

00:38:16.606 --> 00:38:18.730
free trade was superior,
and as a matter of justice

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:20.920
it's required.

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:25.100
I took that view into
2015, and all of a sudden,

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:27.226
here comes along a
presidential candidate who

00:38:27.226 --> 00:38:29.350
I liked what he was saying
on immigration for sure,

00:38:29.350 --> 00:38:30.850
and he made sense to
me on foreign policy,

00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:32.433
but I thought, on
trade he's terrible.

00:38:32.433 --> 00:38:33.220
He's nuts.

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:35.220
He made me go back to
rethink it and look it up,

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:38.580
just to try to figure something
out, revisit the arguments.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:40.962
He changed my mind on
trade-- or he led me

00:38:40.962 --> 00:38:42.670
to the path of changing
my mind on trade,

00:38:42.670 --> 00:38:44.045
and I've come
around to this view

00:38:44.045 --> 00:38:45.350
that trade is not a principle.

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:46.016
It's contextual.

00:38:46.016 --> 00:38:47.740
It's what's good for
us in this moment--

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:50.230
what's good for the United
States, its citizenry,

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:52.750
its communities, its industries,
and so on, in this moment.

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:56.860
There may come a time
when the wide open trading

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:59.080
system that we championed
around the world

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:01.720
in the immediate post-World War
II era is good for us again,

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:03.477
but it isn't right
now, I don't think.

00:39:03.477 --> 00:39:04.810
All of this stuff is contextual.

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:07.480
That's what I say
about prudence.

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:09.580
There's no easy answer to this.

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:12.264
You have to come to it with a
decent understanding of what

00:39:12.264 --> 00:39:14.180
you're trying to achieve
of what the good is--

00:39:14.180 --> 00:39:15.200
what the political good is.

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:16.700
And then you have a debate
with your fellow citizens,

00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:19.050
and you see who wins, and
you see what gets enacted.

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:21.830
So one other short anecdote.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:24.650
A couple of months
ago, I was invited

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:28.099
to give a talk to this small
group of high-achieving people

00:39:28.099 --> 00:39:30.390
in Washington, and they come
in from around the country

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:31.130
and they meet somewhere.

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:32.260
I think the speaker canceled.

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:32.760
That's why.

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:34.940
Oh yeah, I meant to say,
what do you guys all--

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:37.273
it's the All-Star Game, and
you're here listening to me.

00:39:37.273 --> 00:39:39.470
I mean, did you not have
tickets or something?

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:40.350
I feel bad for you.

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:41.870
So they invite me,
and it's a bunch

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:46.760
of real Davos-quality oligarchs
in here, right-- none of them

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:48.200
household names
that I recognize,

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:53.462
but everybody in there is in
some super-rarefied position

00:39:53.462 --> 00:39:54.920
in a corporation
or in the economy,

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:56.503
and they hear me
give my little spiel,

00:39:56.503 --> 00:39:59.550
and they're of course naturally
terrified and horrified.

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:04.110
And this one guy
says to me, but I

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:07.110
run a tech company in San
Bruno, and I can't hire--

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:07.610
you know.

00:40:07.610 --> 00:40:09.560
And I said, well, have you
tried paying them more?

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:10.768
(STAMMERING) Well, you know--

00:40:10.768 --> 00:40:12.660
I-- no, even at
any price, I can't.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:14.530
And then it goes down this road.

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:17.060
So what are you saying--
because I had made the argument,

00:40:17.060 --> 00:40:19.187
we don't need more people
right now, at 347 million

00:40:19.187 --> 00:40:21.770
in the United States, it's not,
at the moment, have a pressing

00:40:21.770 --> 00:40:23.270
need for more people.

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:25.010
And, well, what am I
supposed to do to--

00:40:25.010 --> 00:40:26.426
so how do we decide
this question?

00:40:26.426 --> 00:40:28.490
I said, if the regime
operated the way it's

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:30.650
supposed to operate, and
you were Senator Smith,

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:33.060
and I were Senator Anton,
we'd argue about this.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:34.100
We would debate it.

00:40:34.100 --> 00:40:35.470
You'd tell me why you
thought it was necessary.

00:40:35.470 --> 00:40:37.550
I'd tell you why I thought
it wasn't necessary.

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:38.960
We'd see who could
marshal the most votes.

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:39.560
We would vote on it.

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.018
We would make a
political decision.

00:40:41.018 --> 00:40:42.267
You might win that argument.

00:40:42.267 --> 00:40:43.350
I might win that argument.

00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:45.020
I don't know, but
that's not the ground

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.340
on which we're arguing
about it today,

00:40:46.340 --> 00:40:48.256
and that's what fundamentally
needs to change.

00:40:59.050 --> 00:41:02.950
AUDIENCE: In the present
crisis, how can conservatives

00:41:02.950 --> 00:41:12.280
revitalize politics without
falling into polarization?

00:41:12.280 --> 00:41:17.282
MIKE ANTON: Well, I mean,
polarization amongst ourselves,

00:41:17.282 --> 00:41:17.990
or with the left?

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:19.500
I mean, we're already kind
of polarized with the left.

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:21.880
And their actions have
a lot to do with that,

00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:24.088
and I don't think we're
going to talk them out of it.

00:41:24.088 --> 00:41:26.870
So a certain amount of that is,
if not necessarily, inevitable,

00:41:26.870 --> 00:41:28.260
and we just need
to deal with it.

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:30.980
And in fact, one of the things
that I think conservatives do

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:33.530
is they let themselves
get talked into believing

00:41:33.530 --> 00:41:34.830
that polarization is evil.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:35.960
But what the left
means is, it's evil

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:38.126
when you do it, so just
back down and agree with us.

00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:43.340
There are a lot of
high-minded conservatives

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:45.705
who sort of think that
way-- oh, we can't, it's

00:41:45.705 --> 00:41:47.090
just against my principle.

00:41:47.090 --> 00:41:49.730
Some polarization--
politics is a fight.

00:41:49.730 --> 00:41:54.920
I don't remember the Latin,
but what's that line in Latin?

00:41:54.920 --> 00:41:58.180
Aristotle is accustomed
to seeking a fight, right.

00:41:58.180 --> 00:41:59.180
You see it quoted a lot.

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:00.890
And the point is,
when you're arguing

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:04.030
about important things--

00:42:04.030 --> 00:42:06.090
the noble, and the good,
the just, the true,

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:07.370
the beneficial--

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:09.180
people are going to draw lines,
they're going to take sides,

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:09.950
they're going to get
mad at each other,

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:10.770
and they're going to
yell at each other.

00:42:10.770 --> 00:42:11.630
There's no avoiding it.

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:12.740
You don't want it
to come to violence.

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:14.323
You don't want it
to come to division.

00:42:14.323 --> 00:42:20.256
But if avoiding polarization
is the highest good,

00:42:20.256 --> 00:42:22.630
we're just going to keep on
the trajectory that we're on,

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:24.754
and I don't think that's
good or going to end well.

00:42:33.210 --> 00:42:36.420
AUDIENCE: Thank you so much
for speaking, Mr. Anton.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:38.580
When railing against the
tyranny of expertise,

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:41.610
you said we're losing a
rhetorical war because we make

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:44.040
mistake policy for principle.

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:48.480
It seems to me, though, that
President Trump won in 2016

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:51.150
in large part because of the
list of proposed Supreme Court

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:55.980
nominees, a list provided by
the expert legal establishment

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:58.980
of Conservatism Inc.,
the Federalist Society.

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:01.980
How do you account for the huge
number of conservative voters

00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:05.155
who turned out explicitly
because of that list?

00:43:05.155 --> 00:43:06.780
MIKE ANTON: Because
it was a good list.

00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:08.220
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:08.880
No, seriously.

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:14.180
I'm not saying that there's
no use for expertise.

00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:16.800
But let's replace expertise
with a more important

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:19.190
fundamental word, wisdom.

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:21.270
Expertise is a specific
type of knowledge

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:23.790
that claims to be
wisdom, but that is not.

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:27.720
Of course we always want to be
guided by wisdom, by the truth,

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:29.250
by knowledge of what is good.

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:31.240
Good judges are better
than bad judges.

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:33.600
I would turn to people who
know a whole lot about judges

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:35.430
before making
judicial appointments,

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:36.970
and I would listen
to their advice.

00:43:36.970 --> 00:43:38.970
But the fundamental
point is not that you

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:41.428
don't listen to advice, or that
you don't listen to wisdom,

00:43:41.428 --> 00:43:44.280
or that you don't gather
and process information.

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:46.620
You're not rejecting
those things

00:43:46.620 --> 00:43:48.300
because you're
anti-intellectual,

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:50.130
or you're just
against knowledge.

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:52.897
You're saying that
your claim to expertise

00:43:52.897 --> 00:43:54.230
does not give you the final say.

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:56.250
This is still a fundamentally
political question.

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:57.480
So to take it back
to another one

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:59.640
of those fundamental
tensions I was talking about,

00:43:59.640 --> 00:44:01.290
the final analysis, right--

00:44:01.290 --> 00:44:05.100
the classical argument is that
the only just claim to rule

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:06.120
is wisdom.

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:07.500
And then they
spend a lot of time

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:08.940
undercutting that,
showing you all

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:10.110
the inherent
contradictions, saying, well

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:11.170
that's never going to work.

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:12.530
But you have to agree
with them in principle.

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:14.130
The only just title
to rule is wisdom.

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:15.713
If you know how to
rule, and I don't--

00:44:15.713 --> 00:44:17.700
if you're wise and I'm
not, you should rule me.

00:44:17.700 --> 00:44:21.470
The founders make this point
consistently in The Federalist

00:44:21.470 --> 00:44:23.320
Papers and elsewhere,
where they say,

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:25.936
if there were a perfect
being-- if God directly

00:44:25.936 --> 00:44:28.560
governed the affairs of men, of
course we would let him rule us

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:30.510
without our consent as
a despot, or an angel,

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:31.720
or something like that.

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:34.019
But there's no human
being who has that.

00:44:34.019 --> 00:44:36.060
We have to resolve these
questions for ourselves.

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:38.040
They remain fundamentally
political, and always will.

00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:39.414
That's just in
the nature of man.

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.110
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:52.270
You talked about
the relationship

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:54.070
between the universals
and the particulars,

00:44:54.070 --> 00:44:55.510
and you said that
it was important

00:44:55.510 --> 00:44:57.970
that we don't abandon
the universals

00:44:57.970 --> 00:45:02.000
and simply try to make decisions
based on particular issues.

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:05.770
But in this talk we've heard
about the idea of protection

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:08.380
and the good, and we're
distancing ourselves away

00:45:08.380 --> 00:45:12.880
from traditional principled
movement conservatism in ideas

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:15.250
like the need for
liberty and virtue

00:45:15.250 --> 00:45:19.872
to be combined, or in
terms of natural rights.

00:45:19.872 --> 00:45:21.330
So if we're moving
away from those,

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:25.230
and simply going
after the good, how

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:27.190
do we stay away
from utilitarianism

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:27.982
with that approach?

00:45:27.982 --> 00:45:30.148
MIKE ANTON: I'm not moving
away from natural rights,

00:45:30.148 --> 00:45:31.330
or liberty, or virtue.

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:34.210
I would argue that it's
movement conservatism that's

00:45:34.210 --> 00:45:37.150
forgotten how to properly
interpret these things

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:39.100
and link them together.

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:40.300
That's really my argument.

00:45:40.300 --> 00:45:43.870
When I say they replace
principle with policy,

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:47.310
what I mean is, to them,
policies that might be good,

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:48.430
and that might--

00:45:48.430 --> 00:45:50.680
a great example, a bedrock
principle of the founding,

00:45:50.680 --> 00:45:52.660
is the protection
of property rights--

00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:58.150
hugely important, both as
a utilitarian measure--

00:45:58.150 --> 00:46:00.250
a rich country is a
stronger country--

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:01.999
and as a moral measure.

00:46:01.999 --> 00:46:03.790
The government really
should have the right

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:07.150
to limit your ability to use
your talents to the fullest.

00:46:07.150 --> 00:46:11.410
So they make the case both ways.

00:46:11.410 --> 00:46:13.900
But from there, I think
too many conservatives say,

00:46:13.900 --> 00:46:17.020
OK, we'll take that principle
and we're just going to say,

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:19.060
no limits on acquisition
or on property rights

00:46:19.060 --> 00:46:22.780
can be ever set at all,
because it's a principle.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:26.100
And so the argument against
protectionism in free trade--

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:28.450
or against certain
trade measures is--

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:30.430
one of the arguments
made is not merely--

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:32.180
they make a utilitarian
argument, oh, this

00:46:32.180 --> 00:46:33.819
will never work,
despite the fact

00:46:33.819 --> 00:46:35.110
that it has worked in the past.

00:46:35.110 --> 00:46:35.890
It doesn't mean
it will work now,

00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:37.210
but at least we know it has.

00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:38.860
But they also make
what they think

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:44.260
is a moral argument, which
is that this is somehow

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:46.480
an unjust government
interference in property

00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:48.190
rights.

00:46:48.190 --> 00:46:49.190
I don't see it that way.

00:46:49.190 --> 00:46:52.570
I think that misunderstands what
the founding principles were,

00:46:52.570 --> 00:46:55.330
and what these higher things
that you're talking about are.

00:46:55.330 --> 00:46:56.470
That's my criticism.

00:46:56.470 --> 00:46:59.650
It's not that we're abandoning
natural right, liberty.

00:46:59.650 --> 00:47:01.600
It's that we're
trying to recover them

00:47:01.600 --> 00:47:04.150
in their proper relationship
with one another,

00:47:04.150 --> 00:47:07.195
against a movement that, if it
ever really understood them--

00:47:07.195 --> 00:47:10.300
it maybe did 30 years ago--

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:11.801
it doesn't seem to anymore.

00:47:14.668 --> 00:47:15.543
SPEAKER 1: Back here.

00:47:15.543 --> 00:47:19.350
[INAUDIBLE] Last question.

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:21.230
It better be a good question.

00:47:21.230 --> 00:47:23.170
AUDIENCE: I'll do my best.

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:24.700
Thanks so much
for speaking, sir.

00:47:24.700 --> 00:47:27.116
You began your speech by talking
about the Lincoln-Douglas

00:47:27.116 --> 00:47:29.590
debates, and both candidates
making arguments about history

00:47:29.590 --> 00:47:32.980
to try to convince their voters
of certain first principles.

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:35.590
You have essentially tried
to do that same thing today,

00:47:35.590 --> 00:47:39.220
making an argument about history
of the founding to convince us

00:47:39.220 --> 00:47:40.690
of certain first principles.

00:47:40.690 --> 00:47:42.232
But Stephen Douglas
was not convinced

00:47:42.232 --> 00:47:44.065
by Lincoln's argument,
and there will likely

00:47:44.065 --> 00:47:45.580
be many conservatives
who are not

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:46.810
convinced by your argument--

00:47:46.810 --> 00:47:47.500
MIKE ANTON: Count on it.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:48.610
AUDIENCE: --respectfully.

00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:50.780
I personally happen
to agree with you.

00:47:50.780 --> 00:47:53.320
And even more so, there
are many on the far right

00:47:53.320 --> 00:47:55.810
and the far left who say we
need to reject the tradition

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:57.730
of the founding in general.

00:47:57.730 --> 00:48:00.880
So is there a way to speak
about political values

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:03.400
in a way that can convince
other people who disagree

00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:06.010
with you without
necessarily relying

00:48:06.010 --> 00:48:08.130
on the institution of history?

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:09.880
MIKE ANTON: Well first
thing we need to do

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:13.770
is just banish the word, values,
from this context for reasons

00:48:13.770 --> 00:48:15.550
I can go into later.

00:48:15.550 --> 00:48:18.400
It gets a little abstruse.

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:21.250
So your question
is, can we do this

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:23.920
without appealing to history?

00:48:23.920 --> 00:48:27.640
I mean, look-- maybe, but
why would you want to?

00:48:27.640 --> 00:48:32.770
It's a wonderful tool in
the rhetorical arsenal.

00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:35.626
So we can evaluate the
founders on two bases--

00:48:35.626 --> 00:48:38.250
well, more than two, but just to
say-- are the principles true?

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:41.414
I have examined them with great
diligence for a long time,

00:48:41.414 --> 00:48:43.330
and I believe they are,
and certainly have not

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:44.560
found a better alternative.

00:48:44.560 --> 00:48:45.880
Did they work out in practice?

00:48:45.880 --> 00:48:47.230
Did they deliver
what they said they

00:48:47.230 --> 00:48:49.330
were going to deliver to
us in all of the writings

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:51.288
that they put out in
justifying the revolution?

00:48:51.288 --> 00:48:52.000
They did.

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:53.460
It worked.

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.470
And third, is it ours?

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:57.010
Is it inspiring to us?

00:48:57.010 --> 00:48:58.930
Is it something that
we can look back to?

00:48:58.930 --> 00:49:00.980
A sect or a republic, if
it wishes to live long,

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:02.620
must go back to its beginnings.

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:04.652
We don't go back to the
beginnings of others.

00:49:04.652 --> 00:49:06.610
We go back to what's our
own, because it's part

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:10.420
of human passion is
to love one's own.

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:12.120
It so happens that
our own is good.

00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:13.180
We don't need to
lose sight of it.

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:14.080
We shouldn't lose sight of that.

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:15.640
That's what I mean when I
talk about the universal.

00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:16.630
But it's also our own.

00:49:16.630 --> 00:49:17.530
It's particular.

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:21.970
So I would use them both
in the rhetorical toolkit,

00:49:21.970 --> 00:49:23.470
and do, and will.

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:25.865
I don't see how we gain
anything by giving up

00:49:25.865 --> 00:49:26.740
the one or the other.

00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:31.120
That was the point I was
trying to make at the end by--

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:33.610
some on the
disgruntled right get

00:49:33.610 --> 00:49:35.620
what's wrong with
movement conservatism

00:49:35.620 --> 00:49:38.620
but their view is,
ditch the principle

00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:40.802
and just embrace the
particular alone.

00:49:40.802 --> 00:49:42.760
Ultimately, I don't think
that's going to work,

00:49:42.760 --> 00:49:45.290
and it's unnecessary.

00:49:45.290 --> 00:49:46.310
That's just the flip--

00:49:46.310 --> 00:49:47.330
essentially I think
what they're arguing

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:49.924
for is the flip side of what
you maybe didn't argue for,

00:49:49.924 --> 00:49:50.590
but asked about.

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:57.240
[APPLAUSE]

